From cac22ab53843e16ca3ada39e8102d1f95ab65c84 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuta Saito <kateinoigakukun@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 17:19:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [cmake] Add an option to build without library evolution

A toolchain that links whole programs -- for example a Swift SDK whose
libraries are shipped as LLVM bitcode and compiled with
`-experimental-hermetic-seal-at-link` -- cannot use a resilient testing
library. `-enable-library-evolution` and
`-experimental-hermetic-seal-at-link` are rejected together by the
driver, and the point of the seal is precisely to devirtualize and strip
what resilience must keep indirect and reachable.

Add `SwiftTesting_ENABLE_LIBRARY_EVOLUTION` (default `YES`, so nothing
changes for existing builds). When it is `NO`:

* `Testing`, `_TestDiscovery`, `_TestingInterop` and the overlays are
  compiled without `-enable-library-evolution` and stop emitting
  `.swiftinterface` files;
* `_swift_testing_install_target()` installs the binary `.swiftmodule`
  instead of the `.swiftinterface`. Handing a client an interface built
  without library evolution is not safe -- the client would rebuild the
  module from source and could disagree with the shipped binaries about
  layout and dispatch -- so the module that the libraries were actually
  built from is installed instead. This is the same shape
  swift-corelibs-xctest already uses.
* the `_TestingInternals` Clang module (headers and module map) is
  installed next to the Swift resource directory. A binary
  `.swiftmodule` records every module it imports, `_TestingInternals`
  included, while a `.swiftinterface` never mentions it; without this,
  a client's `import Testing` fails with `missing required module
  '_TestingInternals'`.

Also add `SwiftTesting_DISABLE_SWIFT_SPLIT_COMPILATION`, which keeps
CMP0157 `OLD`. The split-compilation model drives swiftc with an
output-file-map that only names `object` outputs; under `-lto=llvm-full`
or `-lto=llvm-thin` the driver emits `<basename>.bc` in the working
directory instead, the declared objects are never created, and the
archive step fails. The option lets a bitcode build fall back to the
one-step compile-and-archive model, where the driver names its own
outputs. swift-corelibs-foundation has an option of the same shape.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XYudU7MTp4QeCPKenE9XU8
